 These commands ignore `.env.macos` and inherited release settings, use `com.deepseek.harness` as the app ID, and omit automatic-update and mandatory-update configuration. Build tools, standard network proxies, and `DSH_DESKTOP_NPM_REGISTRY` remain available from the shell. They skip Developer ID signing, Apple notarization, temporary signing keychains, and notarization proxy changes. Electron receives a local ad-hoc signature without certificates so the modified executable can run; the DMG is unsigned. Both preparation and the assembled application still run runtime integrity and smoke checks. Local builds omit update feeds, ZIPs, and release completion records, and the upload commands cannot publish them. macOS may require approval in System Settings → Privacy & Security to open an unnotarized application.
 
+<a id="linux-appimage"></a>
+
+## Linux AppImage packaging
+
+Linux is an x64 AppImage target. Build on a Linux x64 host with the locked dependencies installed, a working `tar`, and network access to npm, GitHub release assets and nodejs.org; building does not need FUSE, but running the resulting AppImage does. The packaged runtime and the AppImage carry the same dsh version, and the standard Office payload falls back to the locked WASM engine because the native LibreOffice kit has no Linux distribution.
+
+Build a local unsigned AppImage without `.env.linux`, signing credentials, or update configuration:
+
+```sh
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64:unsigned
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64:unsigned --check
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64:unsigned --dir
+```
+
+The artifact is `apps/desktop/.desktop-build/targets/linux-x64/unsigned-artifacts/deepseek-harness-<version>-linux-x64-unsigned.AppImage`; `--dir` leaves the unpacked `linux-unpacked/` tree in the same directory. These builds use `com.deepseek.harness`, omit `app-update.yml`, a release completion record, and mandatory-update policy. They are for local testing and are not upload inputs.
+
+Build a release-feed AppImage after copying [`.env.linux.example`](../.env.linux.example) to the ignored `.env.linux` and filling the application ID, `DSH_DESKTOP_AUTO_UPDATE_ENV`, the selected download origin and the test release ID (`DOWNLOAD_TEST_RELEASE_ID` for test deployments):
+
+```sh
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64 --check
+pnpm run package:desktop:linux:x64 --build-version 0.2.1-rc.2.20261023.1
+```
+
+The release artifact is in `apps/desktop/.desktop-build/targets/linux-x64/artifacts/` with the unsuffixed name `deepseek-harness-<version>-linux-x64.AppImage`. Packaging writes `app-update.yml` under `linux-unpacked/resources`, the channel metadata `nightly-linux.yml` or `latest-linux.yml`, and `linux-x64-release.json`. Linux has no code-signing or notarization step and no mandatory-update policy; `validateDesktopPackageEnvironment` skips both, while still requiring a valid app ID and, for release builds, an update origin and release ID.
+
+Automatic updates use electron-updater's AppImage updater: the installed application must have `APPIMAGE` set, and the generic feed must serve the channel YAML and the AppImage it names. The AppImage embeds its differential blockmap, so no separate `.blockmap` file is produced or uploaded. Upload a release with `pnpm run upload:linux:x64` (or `pnpm run upload:latest:linux:x64` for the fixed installer URL) after the same `.env.linux` and upload credentials are configured. These are local commands; there is no Linux Actions workflow or CI publication route yet. Windows and macOS upload rules and approval summaries remain unchanged.
+
+On Ubuntu 24.04 and other hosts that restrict unprivileged user namespaces, the generated AppRun script adds `--no-sandbox` when `unshare -Ur true` fails. The desktop entry also passes `--no-sandbox`, because a FUSE-mounted AppImage is `nosuid` and cannot use Electron's setuid `chrome-sandbox`. The application still uses context isolation and its own permission checks, but Chromium's operating-system sandbox is disabled for the AppImage. A desktop that provides AppIndicator can show the tray; GNOME requires its AppIndicator extension.
+
